00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:06.079 align:start position:0%
welcome rain on this blackened land just  west of Sydney it's not the first  downpour since the fire season began  last year but it's reduced the number of  active Blazers and as bringing relief to  areas gripped by the worst drought on  record but the deluge is also sweeping  piles of ash soot and other debris into  rivers lakes and dams that provide  drinking water to those living nearby  we've never had an experience like this  before during the bush pilot period look  we had over three hundred and  eighty-five thousand hectares of the  catchment actually burned and that those  fires actually came down to the water  line so it's a significant impact the  bush fires I've had across there  this is Warragamba dam it supplies 80  percent of Sydney's drinking water but  could be contaminated by charred debris  from the vast bush fires in the area  scientists say it probably won't cause  any health problems but authorities  aren't taking any chances  they've put in silk curtains to catch  any material that could affect water  quality and are monitoring the situation  closely  contaminated water isn't just a problem  for humans small pieces of ash from  trees like this can get into the  waterways and suffocate the fish they  can also cause algal blooms which can  produce dangerous toxins or cut the  amount of oxygen in the water they can  put other wildlife at risk thousands of  fish died in January when brains washed  sludge into the Makai River in New South  Wales and late last year piles of ash  clogged the beaches as fires raged  nearby many bush fires are still burning  up and down this coastline leaving  waterways and water supplies at risk it  takes decades for a forest to regrow and  to re-stabilize we will be dealing with  different quality water different  organic matter coming down higher  sediment loads for many years and I  would predict in many cases certainly in  Sydney's case decades new growth is  beginning to push through the scorched  landscape but the cost of this  unprecedented fire  season is mounting and it's not over yet  Alexia O'Brien al-jazeera New South  Wales Australia
 

